WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.884
When application data needs
confidentiality and

2
00:00:02.884 --> 00:00:05.766
tamper detection, use an established
authenticated encryption

3
00:00:05.766 --> 00:00:08.349
construction supported by a mature
library.

4
00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:10.832
Authenticated encryption with associated
data, often

5
00:00:10.832 --> 00:00:13.714
abbreviated AEAD, accepts plaintext and
optional context

6
00:00:13.714 --> 00:00:16.297
that needs integrity protection without
secrecy.

7
00:00:16.297 --> 00:00:19.674
AES GCM and ChaCha20 Poly1305 are common
examples.

8
00:00:19.674 --> 00:00:22.854
The engineering task is to satisfy the
chosen

9
00:00:22.854 --> 00:00:25.735
construction's contract, not to compare
algorithms casually

10
00:00:25.735 --> 00:00:29.113
or assemble equivalent behavior from lower
level pieces.

11
00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:31.497
Associated data can bind ciphertext to

12
00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:34.776
a tenant, record, purpose, or schema
version.

13
00:00:34.776 --> 00:00:38.452
It remains visible, but a change causes
authentication to

14
00:00:38.452 --> 00:00:42.227
fail when the expected context is supplied
during decryption.

15
00:00:42.227 --> 00:00:45.009
Reconstruct that expected context from the
authorized

16
00:00:45.009 --> 00:00:47.890
operation and trusted records, rather than
accepting

17
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.870
an arbitrary context supplied alongside
the ciphertext.

18
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.851
Define a stable byte encoding for it.

19
00:00:53.851 --> 00:00:56.235
Semantically similar objects with
different serialization

20
00:00:56.235 --> 00:00:58.818
can otherwise produce different
authentication inputs.

21
00:00:58.818 --> 00:01:02.097
Preserve the information needed to
decrypt: an approved

22
00:01:02.097 --> 00:01:06.071
format version, key identifier or version,
nonce, ciphertext, and

23
00:01:06.071 --> 00:01:09.449
authentication tag where the API exposes
it separately.

24
00:01:09.449 --> 00:01:12.827
A format may combine some of these fields.

25
00:01:12.827 --> 00:01:15.211
Binary values can be encoded for

26
00:01:15.211 --> 00:01:18.291
storage, but their encoding adds no
protection.

27
00:01:18.291 --> 00:01:20.675
Validate the record's structure and route

28
00:01:20.675 --> 00:01:22.861
version metadata through controlled
policy.

29
00:01:22.861 --> 00:01:25.245
Stored algorithm names must not enable

30
00:01:25.245 --> 00:01:27.828
arbitrary constructions or unexpected key
access.

31
00:01:27.828 --> 00:01:31.604
Authenticated decryption must either
return verified plaintext or fail.

32
00:01:31.604 --> 00:01:34.783
Some low level streaming APIs produce
provisional bytes

33
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:37.664
before the final authentication check; do
not

34
00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:41.042
release those bytes to callers or side
effects.

35
00:01:41.042 --> 00:01:45.612
Prefer a wrapper that enforces this
boundary and bounds record size.

36
00:01:45.612 --> 00:01:49.586
Successful authentication does not
establish that the requester may read

37
00:01:49.586 --> 00:01:54.255
the record, nor does it automatically
prove freshness or prevent replay.

38
00:01:54.255 --> 00:01:56.640
Enforce application permissions separately
and add

39
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.826
state rules if replay matters.

40
00:01:58.826 --> 00:02:01.210
The result is a protected representation

41
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.197
with clear cryptographic guarantees and

42
00:02:03.197 --> 00:02:05.780
clearly identified responsibilities
outside those guarantees.

